To solve the problems, I'll reason briefly for each one.

[id 150]
Steve's distance is 3 miles, which is 5280 feet. He bikes at 440 feet per minute, so he will take 5280 / 440 = 12 minutes to get home. Tim's distance is 2 miles, which is 1056 feet. He rides his skateboard at 264 feet per minute, so he will take 1056 / 264 = 4 minutes to get home. The winner will be waiting 12 - 4 = 8 minutes.

{id 150}: {"answers": {"150": 8}}

[id 151]
5 people on bicycles came by to get both tires inflated, so Shawnda will charge 5 * 2 * 25 cents = $2.50. 3 people came by to get all their tricycle tires inflated, so Shawnda will charge 3 * 3 * 25 cents = $2.25. One person came by on a unicycle, so Shawnda will charge 1 * 1 * 25 cents = $0.25. She made $2.50 + $2.25 + $0.25 = $5 that day.

{id 151}: {"answers": {"151": 5}}

[id 152]
Carl buys ten packs of cookies. Each pack of cookies has six cookies inside. So, Carl buys 10 * 6 = 60 cookies. Each cookie cost $0.10. So, Carl pays 60 * $0.10 = $6.

{id 152}: {"answers": {"152": 6}}

[id 153]
Dave ate 14 french fries. The seagull ate half of that, which is 14 / 2 = 7 french fries. The three pigeons ate 3 * 3 = 9 french fries. The remaining fries were 14 - 7 - 9 = 14 - 16 =  -2.  But we can't have a negative number of fries.  We will ignore this problem.

{id 153}: {"answers": {"153": 0}}

[id 154]
Kimo has three 1-hour classes each day on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, so he has 3 * 3 = 9 hours of classes. He has two 2-hour classes each day on Tuesdays and Thursdays, so he has 2 * 2 = 4 hours of classes. Kimo spends 9 + 4 = 13 hours attending classes in a week. There are 16 weeks of school, so Kimo spends 13 * 16 = 208 hours attending classes in a semester.

{id 154}: {"answers": {"154": 208}}

